# We can express a pair as: (\z. z a b)
# Where a is the first value or pair and b is second.
#
# To get the values out, we can apply this to true or false since they return first or second arg.
# (\z. z a b)True  -> a
# (\z. z a b)False -> b
#
# Now, if we want a predecessor function; will decrease a number by 1.
#
# We will use pairs to have pair of adjacent numbers:
#     (0, 0), (1, 0), (2, 1), (3, 2)
#     Then, if we want to predecess any number, we can just get it's second member.
#     We need a function that maps (n, n - 1) to (n + 1, n); zero is starting condition
#
# Our initial pair is: (0, 0);
# Our increment function here will be: (\p.\z. z (S (p T)) (p T)); T = true = \x.\y. x
#     It takes a pair and returns a pair
#     (\p.\z. z (S (p T)) (p T))
#       | [___________________]
#       |         |-------------- A pair ( with S(p T) and p T as elements)
#       +------------------------ Parameter pair
#
#         p T -> first member of pair in the argument.
#
#     For returned pair:
#         1st member (S(p T)): incremented 1st member of argument ; (n + 1)
#         2nd member (p T)   : 1st member of argument             ; (n)
#
# If I apply predecessor function to 0 n times and yield second member, I get 'n - 1'

(\f.\x. f (f (f x)))             # Applying 3 to predecessor
    (\p.\z.                      # Predecessor
        z
          ((\m.\f.\x. f (m f x)) # Sucessor
             (p (\a.\b. a)))     # p T
          (p (\a.\b. a)))        # p T
    (\s.                         # (0, 0)
        s
          (\f.\x. x)
          (\f.\x. x))
    (\a.\b. b)                   # applying to false to get second element
